'''block<sup>2</sup>''' (blok), ''n.'' [In this sense the noun, in E., is in most senses due rather to the verb: see ''block''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'' The orig. noun is found once in ME. ''blok'', an inclosed space; cf. OF. ''bloc'', barrier, post, wall (> OF. ''bloquer'', F. ''bloquer'', stop, block: see the verb; the mod. F. ''bloc'' goes with ''block''<sup>1</sup>); MD. ''block'', post, stocks (cf. ''blocklands'', an inclosed piece of ground, ditch, swamp, MLG. ''block'', post, stocks, LG. ''blokland'', an inclosed swamp), = OFries. *''blokk'', in comp. ''block-syl'', a sluice; OHG. ''biloh'', confinement (MHG. ''bloch'', a kind of trap, G. ''block'', stocks, prison), < ''bi-'', = AS. ''bi-'', ''be-'', E. ''be-''<sup>1</sup>, + ''loh'', MHG. G. ''loch'', a confined space, hole, dungeon, = AS. ''loc'', E. ''lock'', a place shut in, etc.: see ''lock''<sup>1</sup>. Confused more or less with the forms cited under ''block''<sup>1</sup>, with which it is by some identified. See the verb following.] '''1.''' Any obstruction or cause of obstruction; a stop; a hindrance; an obstacle.

